<p>The United States and Iran announced a temporary ceasefire arrangement, and markets quickly rallied as investors bet the truce could lower the risk of a wider regional disruption. Several reports said the arrangement raised hopes that the Strait of Hormuz could reopen or that tanker traffic might resume through the vital shipping route, whose disruption had driven energy prices higher and rattled global markets. Oil prices fell sharply, with widely traded crude contracts dropping to around or below $95 a barrel after earlier war-driven gains, while US equities rallied and the Dow Jones Industrial Average posted its strongest day in about a year. That offered a hopeful sign for consumers and businesses worried about energy costs, though multiple sources cautioned that the ceasefire remained fragile and that risks to shipping and renewed conflict had not disappeared.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/USTNJEJMbQK6JMnB8tSO-gMcjktB_CUqEZKgiVtxJuIMSB7qSdbb5gj8Vw4etIMumnZvYWd65ghNqhzQWLsObYrasB106PpUV9TsDH0740ZacPU8LSE4vNDXwi3SHNKjHHhD1RfQSHOndJmKnsuKRDrv7xxlXUeUX-AXHN5HRWV_VANPAbeG1JLe8fMGzfaoAxGb-EXFjftTDbds8VLZQ1Mo0fBK2zjwDg' alt='View of the Strait of Hormuz, the key waterway at the center of market concerns' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Futures jump: After the ceasefire announcement, S&amp;P 500 futures rose 2.2% and Dow futures climbed about 930 points in early after-hours trading, an immediate sign of investor optimism before the full market session.</li><li>Oil retreat: Al-Monitor reported that the most widely traded oil contracts fell about 15%, reversing part of the spike that had built during more than a month of fighting.</li><li>Dollar moves: Alongside the rally in shares, the US dollar retreated as traders moved away from the defensive positioning that had accompanied fears of a broader war.</li><li>Relief pattern: Semafor described Wednesday&#x27;s rebound as a familiar market pattern, with investors quickly returning to risk assets when geopolitical tensions appear to cool.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Shipping-focused coverage: Reports linking the ceasefire to a possible reopening of the Strait of Hormuz suggested the market bounce was driven mainly by expectations for tanker access, not by any belief that regional tensions had fully faded. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-08-0700/economy-and-jobs-oil-prices-plunge-and-shares-jump-us-iran-ceasefire-plan'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>Analysts cited by Al-Monitor: Market strategists said the temporary truce replaced immediate fears of a devastating escalation and helped risk appetite recover. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/oil-prices-plunge-stocks-surge-us-iran-ceasefire'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>PBS NewsHour&#x27;s caution: Even after the rebound, stocks remained below where they traded before the war and oil stayed elevated, showing that only part of the conflict premium had been removed. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/economy/oil-plunges-below-95-as-dow-surges-1100-following-iran-ceasefire-deal'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/economy/oil-plunges-below-95-as-dow-surges-1100-following-iran-ceasefire-deal'>Oil plunges below $95 as Dow surges 1,100 following Iran ceasefire deal</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-08-0700/economy-and-jobs-oil-prices-drop-and-stock-futures-jump-us-and-iran-agree-2-week-ceasefire'>Oil prices drop and stock futures jump as US and Iran agree to a 2-week ceasefire</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/08/2026/us-stocks-rally-in-familiar-pattern'>US stocks rally in familiar pattern</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/oil-prices-plunge-stocks-surge-us-iran-ceasefire'>Oil prices plunge, stocks surge on US-Iran ceasefire</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2026/04/08/trump-threatens-tariffs-countries-supplying-weapons-iran-ceasefire.html'>Oil prices plunge below US$95 and the Dow Jones Industrial Average surges past 1,000 after the ceasefire announcement.</a> - cnbc.com</li></ul>